      "textEn": "It was to be taken to the pass. When a person commits murder, he becomes a sinner, therefore his\nalso should be killed, otherwise after death he will have to suffer all the consequences of sin. Similarly,\nThe punishment given by Yamraj to extremely disgraceful sinful people is the method of purification. Therefore,\nthe messengers of Yama pleaded with the messengers of Vishnu not to stop Ajamila from being taken to Yamraj. Thus, under the sixth canto of Srimad Bhagavat, \"The History of Ajamila's Life\"\nThe Bhakti Vedanta meaning of the first chapter named \"Bhakti\" is completed. Chapter Two.\n\nThe salvation of Ajamila by the messengers of Vishnu.\n\nIn this chapter, the messengers of Vaikuntha, the messengers of Yama, tell the glory of chanting the holy name of God. Vishnu's messengers said, \"Now, even in the assembly of devotees, impure deeds are being performed,\nbecause the person who is not worthy of punishment is going to be punished in the assembly of Yamraj (the god of death).\"\nPeople\nare helpless and they depend on the government for their safety, but if the government,\ntaking advantage of this, harms the citizens, then where will they go? We are watching carefully\nthat Ajamila should not be punished, although you people are trying to take him to Yamraj to punish him.\"\n\nAjamila became blameworthy only by singing the glory of the name of the Supreme Personality of Godhead through his own means. Vishnu's messengers explained it as follows: \"This Brahmin, by uttering the name of Narayan only once, has been freed from the fruits of sinful life. He has not only been freed from the sins of this birth,\nbut has also been freed from the sins of many thousands of other lives. He has first\nHe has atoned for his sins, whoever atones according to the instructions of the scriptures,"
